          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 6,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Yes, that would be helpful! Also, how do I choose the right width and height for the handlebars if I ride both in the city and on forest trails?",
          "user_rubrik": "Could you tell me about handlebars?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Flat bars are common on mountain bikes and provide a low, aggressive position, but may not be ideal for comfort or back pain. Riser bars have an upward sweep, allowing a more upright posture, which reduces strain on your back and wrists\u2014making them a great choice for comfort and casual riding on both city streets and rough trails [clueweb22-en0023-62-08521:1][clueweb22-en0031-94-19558:0]. Alternative comfort or 'alt bars' (like Jones or Surly Moloko) offer additional backsweep and rise, further enhancing comfort and wrist alignment [clueweb22-en0015-99-08241:1]. For width, wider bars (720\u2013760mm) provide better control on trails, while not being too wide for city navigation. Handlebar height (rise) should be set so you can ride comfortably upright, which is especially important for back pain\u2014experiment with different rises and consider a shorter stem if needed [clueweb22-en0001-50-03380:15].",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0023-62-08521:1": 0.06772040419022898,
            "clueweb22-en0001-50-03380:15": 0.05651739296979273,
