        "turn": 6,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Those are solid considerations! What about maintenance tips for sustaining the quality of grips or tape?",
          "user_rubrik": "What are the key things to look for on mountain bike pedals?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 0,
          "text": "To keep your grips or handlebar tape comfortable and long-lasting\u2014without frequent replacements\u2014focus on these low-effort, sustainable maintenance tips:\n\n**For Grips (Flat Bars):**\n- **Wipe Down Regularly:** Use a damp cloth to remove sweat, dirt, and grime. This prevents material breakdown and keeps them grippy.\n- **Check for Movement:** If grips twist or slide, have a shop re-secure or replace them with lock-on types\u2014these last longer and are easy for a mechanic to swap.\n- **Inspect for Wear:** Look for cracks, hard spots, or loss of cushioning. Upgrading to ergonomic or gel grips can extend comfort and lifespan.\n\n**For Tape (Drop Bars):**\n- **Clean Gently:** Wipe with mild soap and water, especially after wet or muddy rides. Avoid harsh cleaners that degrade materials.\n- **Monitor Padding:** If tape feels thin or loses its tackiness, consider adding a second layer for extra comfort before replacing.\n- **Annual Refresh:** Even with care, tape should be replaced about once a year for hygiene and comfort",
