          "user_utterance": "These tips will help maintain grip quality! How do I know when to replace my bike's tires?",
          "user_rubrik": "What are the key things to look for on mountain bike pedals?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 0,
          "text": "To know when your bike tires need replacing\u2014especially as a comfort-focused, sustainability-minded rider\u2014watch for these crucial signs:\n\n**1. Tread Wear Indicators:**  \n- Most tires have small rubber bars (\"wear bars\") molded into the tread grooves. If the tread is worn down to these bars (about 2/32\" depth), grip and safety are compromised\u2014especially on wet or rough paths.\n\n**2. Visible Damage:**  \n- Look for cracks, cuts, or bulges in the tire sidewall or tread. These can lead to sudden flats or blowouts.\n\n**3. Frequent Flats:**  \n- If you\u2019re getting more punctures, the rubber may be too thin to protect against debris.\n\n**4. Loss of Comfort:**  \n- Worn tires lose shock absorption, making rides bumpier\u2014important for your comfort on rough terrain.\n\n**Sustainability Tip:**  \n- If tread is only lightly worn and there\u2019s no damage, you can sometimes extend life by rotating front and rear tires (ask a shop to do this).\n\nWould you like tips on choosing a tire",
