      {
        "turn": 6,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Are clipless pedals worth considering on a mountain bike?",
          "user_rubrik": "Are clipless pedals worth considering on a mountain bike?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "For your casual mountain biking style on rough terrain, prioritizing comfort and managing back pain, clipless pedals offer foot stability and efficient power transfer (P1, P3, P9). However, they come with a learning curve for quick disengagement (P4, P12), which can be a safety concern on technical trails where you might need to put a foot down quickly. Getting stuck could lead to falls (P8, P17), potentially aggravating back pain. While good for competitive riding, their main benefits often align with speed rather than comfort (P18). Hybrid pedals (P5, P13, P14) offer a versatile alternative.",
          "citations": {
