      {
        "turn": 6,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Yeah, grip is important\u2014sometimes my shoes get wet or muddy! Are there pedal types that are better for bikes going in and out of buses and trains a lot?",
          "user_rubrik": "What are the key things to look for on mountain bike pedals?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "For your use case, where you frequently transport your bike on buses and trains and need reliable grip in wet/muddy conditions on trails, **flat (or platform) pedals** are often the best choice.\n\n*   **Convenience:** Flat pedals allow you to wear any comfortable shoes, which is ideal for easily getting on and off public transport without needing to change into specific cycling shoes.\n*   **Grip:** Look for flat pedals with numerous **traction pins or metal studs**. These provide excellent grip, preventing your feet from slipping even when your shoes are wet or muddy on forest trails and rough paths.\n\nAlternatively, **hybrid pedals** (flat on one side, clipless on the other) offer versatility if you want the option to clip in sometimes while still having a flat side for casual rides or transport.",
