    "responses": [
      {
        "turn": 6,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Are clipless pedals worth considering on a mountain bike?",
          "user_rubrik": "Are clipless pedals worth considering on a mountain bike?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "short answer : yes \u2014 clipless can be worth it for control on climbs, rocky roots, and rough paths \u2014 but they \u2019 re not mandatory. given your comfort - first, casual style, consider low - commitment options. good ways to try clipless with minimal downside - hybrid pedals : clipless on one side, flat on the other. easy for transit and errands in regular shoes. \u2022 shimano pd - eh500 ( city / trail ), pd - m324 ( durable classic ) - mud - shedding, easy in / out clipless : great for forest and wet riding. \u2022 crankbrothers candy or mallet, time atac ( forgiving entry / exit, good in mud ) - keep it friendly : 4 \u2013 6\u00b0 float, low release tension ; if using spd, choose multi \u2011 release cleats ( sh56 ). why you might prefer flats sometimes - winter boots / snow or lots of stop \u2011 start riding - lowest maintenance and simplest to use upgrade - friendly tip - if you do go spd, inexpensive platform adapters can convert them to flats when you want to ride in sneakers \u2014 no pedal swap needed. setup matters - cleats slightly rearward ( near midfoot",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0037-95-18420:0": 0.47475528717041016,
